==================================== INPUT ====================================
// Test: a QUERY-ROOTED resolver whose `@rootFragment` reads server data but which
// does NOT declare `@returnFragment` is REJECTED under `@exec_time_resolvers`.
//
// This fixture used to pin a routing gap rather than an error. The flag
// `has_server_to_client_resolvers` was not set for a Query parent unless the
// resolver was a magic fragment, so `LSRelayEnvironmentConfig` never routed the
// operation to `executeWithNetwork`; it landed on the legacy path where the
// resolver and the network run as two concurrent subscriptions and the root
// fragment is evaluated against an empty store, silently yielding null.
//
// The gap is now closed by rejection instead of by routing. The S2C identity-only
// rule used to carve `Query` out, which is what let this shape compile at all;
// with the carve-out gone the operation no longer builds, so there is nothing
// left to mis-route. Rejecting is the safe direction: the runtime could not have
// served this correctly, and a compile error says so instead of returning null.
//
// The magic-fragment case is unaffected — `exec_time_resolver_query_root_shadow_routes`
// still compiles and still sets the flag, because the transplant puts the server
// selections into the operation and the shadow corridor permits that path.
//
// Reading a server field from a Query-rooted root fragment becomes legal again
// only once the field is projectable from the normalized payload, which is what
// the `@UNSTABLE_key` work adds.

//- NeedsRoutingQuery.js
graphql`query NeedsRoutingQuery @exec_time_resolvers {
  server_reading_greeting
}`

//- Query_server_reading_greeting.js
/**
 * @relayField Query.server_reading_greeting: String
 * @rootFragment QueryServerReadingGreetingFragment
 */
graphql`fragment QueryServerReadingGreetingFragment on Query {
  me {
    name
  }
}`

//- relay.config.json
{
  "language": "typescript",
  "schema": "schema.graphql",
  "featureFlags": {
    "enable_exec_time_resolvers_directive": true
  }
}

//- schema.graphql
type Query {
  me: User
}

type User {
  name: String
}
==================================== OUTPUT ===================================
✖︎ Server-to-client resolver @rootFragment `QueryServerReadingGreetingFragment` runs at exec time, where it is projected from the normalized response rather than read from the store. Found disallowed selection: `me`. Allowed here: identity (`__typename`, `id`, `__id`), a field granted by `@UNSTABLE_key(fields: "...")` on the type that declares it, and the selections the resolver's own magic fragment transplants. Granting `me` asserts it is stable for the record's lifetime and always arrives in the same payload as the record; where that does not hold, read the field at read time instead.

  Query_server_reading_greeting.js:6:3
    5 │ fragment QueryServerReadingGreetingFragment on Query {
    6 │   me {
      │   ^^
    7 │     name
